Your function calls itself on every path, which means it will continue to call itself until the base case is reached. The base case for a factorial function is when n is 0, because 0! is 1. In your function, `n * fact(n - 1)` will keep calling itself until `n` is 0, at which point it will stop calling itself and return 1. Your function will then multiply this 1 by itself `n` times, which will be the factorial of `n`.

To fix this, you need to add a base case to your function. Here's the corrected code:

```cpp
#include <iostream>

// Return n! using recursion.
int fact(int n) {
    // Base case: if n is 0, return 1
    if (n == 0) {
        return 1;
    }
    // Recursive case: n * fact(n - 1)
    return n * fact(n - 1);
}

int main() {
    std::cout << fact(5) << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

Now, when you call `fact(5)`, it will correctly return 120, which is 5!

